                    clock_r3:               `Months 1–9 · Pathway design`,
                    clock_r4:               `Month 9 · Renewal pitch`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the capability gaps`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Aurora’s independent evaluation, the sponsored managers’ role profiles, and the current content-led curriculum are on the table. <strong>Diagnose why the programme produces completion without transfer</strong>, then decide where to start. The instinct is to refine or add modules — but the curriculum is built <em>forward from content</em> (“here are the modules”) rather than <em>backward from capability</em> (“here is what these managers can’t yet do”). Map the real workplace gaps and tie each to an outcome a boss could actually observe.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Blend academic and workplace learning`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Capability develops roughly <strong>70% on the job, 20% through others, 10% in formal courses</strong> — yet CMDC runs 70% classroom, inverting the model. Rebalance the blend and, crucially, <strong>integrate</strong> the two halves so theory is applied to a live problem and reflection feeds back into the theory. Two streams sharing a calendar is not integration. And remember the constraint: managers gave you <em>~6 hours a week</em>, not twelve.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence the development pathway`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Order the 9 months so each academic block feeds a workplace application, reflection consolidates it, and assessment evidences transfer rather than recall. The decisive variable is the one most designs forget: <strong>the learner’s line manager</strong>. Transfer happens in the workplace — if the boss never sponsors the change, protects no time and rewards the old behaviour, the most elegant pathway fails on contact with the job.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Evidence the uplift & win back the sponsor`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Aurora’s CHRO wants proof of <strong>capability uplift and transfer</strong> — not another completion certificate. Design the measurement, protect the 88% completion rate <em>without</em> reverting to recall exams, and build a renewal proposition around employer-relevant value. Completion was never the problem; transfer was. Make the case in the CHRO’s language: what changed on the job, and how do you know?`,

                    r1_start_label:         `How do you begin the redesign?`,
                    start_content:          `Start from the existing modules — refine the content we already teach; it has worked for years`,
                    start_addmodules:       `Add two new academic modules — the transfer gap means managers need more knowledge`,
                    start_gapmap:           `Start from Aurora’s evaluation — map what these managers actually can’t yet do on the job, then design backward`,
                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `Your root-cause diagnosis of the 88%-complete / 38%-transfer gap.`,
                    diag_ability:           `Managers aren’t capable enough yet — we must raise the academic bar`,
                    diag_motivation:        `Managers aren’t motivated — add exams and stricter assessment to make them try harder`,
                    diag_system:            `A systems failure — the programme is content-led, the classroom doesn’t connect to the job, and the workplace never reinforces it`,
                    r1_lens_label:          `Through which lens do you frame the capabilities to build?`,
                    lens_recall:            `Recall of frameworks — managers should know the theory cold`,
                    lens_fixed:             `A fixed competency checklist — tick the standard set of management skills`,
                    lens_systems:           `Adaptive, systems-thinking capability — sense-making for a beyond-VUCA world, tied to live workplace outcomes`,
                    r1_depth_label:         `How deeply do you map each gap to a measurable workplace outcome? (%)`,
                    r1_depth_readout:       `Gaps linked to a concrete on-the-job outcome:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,

                    r2_blend_label:         `Set the learning blend — formal / social / on-the-job (the 70-20-10 lever).`,
                    blend_classroom:        `Keep 70% classroom / 30% workplace — protect academic rigour, it is what we are known for`,
                    blend_more_class:       `Push to 80% classroom — more taught input is the safest way to lift capability`,
                    blend_balanced:         `Rebalance toward ~50/50 with structured workplace application — formal teaches the frame, the job builds the capability`,
                    blend_all_job:          `Go 90% on-the-job — almost drop the classroom and let experience do everything`,
                    r2_integration_label:   `How do the academic and workplace halves connect?`,
                    integration_parallel:   `Run them as two parallel streams sharing a calendar — classroom on Tuesdays, a separate workplace project alongside`,
                    integration_sequential: `Teach all the theory first, then send them off to apply it at the end`,
                    integration_integrated: `Integrate them — each concept is applied to a live problem in the manager’s own organisation, and reflection feeds back into the theory`,
                    r2_social_label:        `Add the “20” — coaching, peer learning and reflection.`,
                    social_none:            `Skip it — coaching is a nice-to-have we can’t afford in the time budget`,
                    social_optional:        `Offer optional peer forums — available, but not built into the pathway`,
                    social_built_in:        `Build in structured coaching, action-learning sets and guided reflection as a core modality`,
                    r2_time_label:          `Learner hours/week you design the blend to consume (budget is ~6).`,
                    r2_time_readout:        `Hours/week the design assumes:`,
                    r2_hrs:                 `h`,
                    r2_time_hint:           `Managers hold full jobs and gave you ~6 hours. Design above 6 and the pathway won’t actually happen — engagement, and your 88% completion, will fall. Design is allocation under constraint.`,

                    r3_sequence_label:      `Sequence the 9-month pathway.`,
                    seq_frontload:          `Front-load all teaching, then assess at the end — the traditional academic shape`,
                    seq_blocks:             `Stand-alone monthly blocks — each a self-contained topic with no carry-over`,
                    seq_spiral:             `Spiral cycles — each academic block feeds a workplace application, reflection consolidates it, the next block builds on the evidence`,
                    r3_manager_label:       `The role of the learner’s line manager in the pathway.`,
                    mgr_ignore:             `Leave them out — our relationship is with the learner, not their boss`,
                    mgr_inform:             `Send the boss a syllabus for information — keep them in the loop, nothing more`,
                    mgr_engage:             `Engage them as sponsors — agree the application project, protect learner time, and have them confirm changed behaviour`,
                    r3_gate_label:          `What does each stage gate require to advance?`,
                    gate_exam:              `A written exam — proves the manager learned the material`,
                    gate_attendance:        `Attendance and participation — they showed up and engaged`,
                    gate_deliverable:       `A workplace deliverable — evidence of the capability applied to a real problem at work`,
                    r3_touch_label:         `Learner–manager touchpoints across the 9 months (number).`,
                    touch_0:                `0 — the manager is never formally involved`,
                    touch_2:                `2 — a kickoff and a closing review`,
                    touch_4:                `4 — quarterly check-ins tied to each application milestone`,

                    r4_measure_label:       `How will you evidence the outcome to Aurora?`,
                    measure_completion:     `Report completion and satisfaction scores — they were always strong`,
                    measure_self:           `Add learner self-ratings of confidence — quick and cheap to collect`,
                    measure_transfer:       `Measure transfer directly — manager-confirmed behaviour change and workplace-outcome evidence, baselined and re-measured`,
                    r4_protect_label:       `Protecting the 88% completion rate while you chase transfer.`,
                    protect_drop:           `Let completion slide — transfer is all Aurora cares about now`,
                    protect_harden:         `Harden the exams so only the strongest pass — completion as a quality filter`,
                    protect_align:          `Keep completion high by making the workplace deliverables themselves the assessment — passing and transferring become the same act`,
                    r4_pitch_label:         `Your renewal pitch to Aurora’s CHRO.`,
                    pitch_rigour:           `Lead with academic rigour and our brand — we are a respected business school`,
                    pitch_discount:         `Lead with price — offer a discount to keep the contract`,
                    pitch_value:            `Lead with employer-relevant value — show the projected capability uplift, the transfer evidence framework, and the gaps each phase closes for Aurora’s managers`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that transfer — not completion — is the true output of management development? (%)`,

                    warn_depth_range:       `Gap-mapping depth must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_time_range:        `Designed learner hours/week must be a sensible number of hours.`,
                    warn_sequence:          `Choose how you sequence the pathway before committing.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Design choice recorded.`,
                    coach_r1_gapmap:        `<strong>That is the gap-led move.</strong> Starting from Aurora’s evaluation and the managers’ real workplace gaps means you design <em>backward</em> from capability, not forward from content. When the CHRO asks “which of our managers’ specific gaps does week 4 close?”, you now have an answer — and a programme aligned to employer-relevant outcomes.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>Refining the existing modules keeps you designing forward from content.</strong> You’ve sequenced a beautiful set of topics — but you never asked what Aurora’s managers actually can’t do today. Until the design starts from the capability gap, you’ll keep producing completion without transfer.`,
                    coach_r1_addmodules:    `<strong>Aurora said managers pass but don’t change — and your fix is two more academic modules.</strong> You’ve pushed the blend further toward the classroom and cut workplace-application time. Capability develops ~70% on the job; you just moved the design the opposite way. Why would <em>more</em> of what isn’t transferring fix transfer?`,
                    coach_r1_ability:       `<strong>“Managers aren’t capable enough” misreads the data.</strong> They pass at 88% — knowledge isn’t the bottleneck. The gap is between knowing and <em>doing differently on the job</em>. Raising the academic bar deepens the part that already works and ignores the part that doesn’t.`,
                    coach_r1_motivation:    `<strong>Stricter exams answer a motivation problem you don’t have.</strong> Managers complete; they just don’t transfer. Harder recall assessment risks the 88% completion and still evidences nothing about changed behaviour. The system, not the learner’s effort, is producing the gap.`,
                    coach_r1_recall:        `<strong>Framing capability as recall is the heart of the problem.</strong> Managers can already recite the frameworks — that is why they pass. Beyond-VUCA management needs adaptive, sense-making capability tied to live work, not theory held cold for an exam.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a systems failure, framed capability as adaptive systems-thinking, and started backward from Aurora’s real gaps. That is gap-led design — and it sets up every later decision to actually move transfer rather than polish completion.`,

                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That rebalance honours how managers actually learn.</strong> Roughly 70% on the job, 20% through others, 10% formal — moving toward ~50/50 with structured workplace application puts capability-building where it develops while keeping the academic frame that makes it coherent. Transfer has room to move now.`,
                    coach_r2_classroom:     `<strong>Keeping 70% classroom preserves the very design that produced the gap.</strong> It is what CMDC is known for — and it inverts the 70-20-10 logic. The classroom is where managers already succeed; the job is where they don’t change. Leaving the blend untouched leaves the transfer gap untouched.`,
                    coach_r2_moreclass:     `<strong>80% classroom doubles down on the inversion.</strong> You answered a transfer problem with more of the content that isn’t transferring, and squeezed workplace-application time even harder. Aurora will recognise this immediately as the opposite of an “embedded, work-based” model — which is exactly what the competitor is offering.`,
                    coach_r2_alljob:        `<strong>90% on-the-job over-corrects.</strong> Experience builds capability, but with almost no formal frame managers lack the concepts to make sense of it — and the academic completion your accreditation rests on collapses. The 70-20-10 model is a blend, not a single dial pushed to the extreme.`,
                    coach_r2_parallel:      `<strong>Two streams sharing a calendar is not integration — it’s two programmes on one timetable.</strong> Shelley’s pedagogy means the theory is <em>applied to</em> the live problem and reflection feeds back into the theory. Where do the academic and workplace halves actually touch?`,
                    coach_r2_sequential:    `<strong>Teaching everything first, then applying at the end, breaks the feedback loop.</strong> By the time managers reach the workplace task, the concept is cold and the reflection never returns to the theory. Integration is iterative — apply as you learn, not after.`,
                    coach_r2_overbook:      `<strong>Your redesign assumes more time than managers have.</strong> They hold full jobs and gave you ~6 hours a week. Above that, half the pathway won’t happen — engagement drops and your 88% completion falls with it. Design is allocation under constraint. What did you cut to make the time budget real?`,

                    coach_r3_frontload:     `<strong>Front-loading teaching, then assessing at the end, is the academic shape that produced the gap.</strong> The application comes too late to feed the theory, and assessment tests recall, not transfer. Spiral the pathway so each block is applied and consolidated before the next.`,
                    coach_r3_ignoremgr:     `<strong>You left out the most important variable.</strong> The learner goes back to a boss who never sponsored the change, protects no time, and rewards the old behaviour. Transfer happens in the workplace, not the classroom — design the line manager in, or the pathway fails on contact with the job.`,
                    coach_r3_inform:        `<strong>A syllabus for information isn’t engagement.</strong> The boss reading an email doesn’t protect learner time, agree the application project, or confirm changed behaviour. Transfer needs the manager as an active sponsor, not a cc line.`,
                    coach_r3_exam:          `<strong>A written exam evidences recall, not changed behaviour.</strong> You can protect the pass rate this way and prove nothing about transfer — which was the whole problem. Make the gate a workplace deliverable so advancing <em>is</em> evidence of the capability applied.`,
                    coach_r3_pathway:       `<strong>That is a transfer-built pathway.</strong> Spiral cycles that apply each block, a line manager engaged as sponsor protecting time and confirming change, and stage gates that demand a real workplace deliverable — assessment now evidences transfer instead of recall, and the workplace conditions for transfer are designed in.`,

                    coach_r4_value:         `<strong>That is the renewal the CHRO will buy.</strong> You measured transfer directly, kept completion high by making the deliverables the assessment, and pitched employer-relevant value — projected uplift, an evidence framework, and the specific gaps each phase closes. You’re answering Aurora in its own language: changed behaviour on the job, proven.`,
                    coach_r4_self:          `<strong>Self-ratings are cheap and weak evidence.</strong> Learners feeling more confident isn’t the same as managers confirming changed behaviour — and a results-focused CHRO knows the difference. Baseline and re-measure transfer with the workplace as the witness.`,
                    coach_r4_completiononly:`<strong>Reporting completion and satisfaction again misses the point.</strong> Those were always strong — and they’re exactly what Aurora’s evaluation dismissed. Completion was never the problem; transfer was. Measuring it won’t answer the question the CHRO actually asked.`,
                    coach_r4_harden:        `<strong>Hardening the exams to filter quality torches your completion rate.</strong> You protect rigour on paper and hand Aurora a lower pass rate <em>and</em> no transfer evidence. Completion and transfer aren’t a trade-off if the assessment is the workplace deliverable.`,
                    coach_r4_drop:          `<strong>Letting completion slide trades one problem for another.</strong> Aurora cares about transfer, yes — but a collapsing pass rate signals a programme that stopped working, not one that got serious about the job. Protect both by aligning assessment to the real outcome.`,
                    coach_r4_rigour:        `<strong>Leading with rigour and brand is answering a question Aurora didn’t ask.</strong> Their evaluation already conceded you’re academically strong — and still moved to walk. The competitor’s pitch is “embedded, work-based.” Match it on employer-relevant value, not prestige.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>Leading with price concedes the argument.</strong> A discount says “our value is the same, just cheaper” — when the whole problem was value, not cost. Aurora will take the discount and still move the contract the moment a work-based rival proves transfer.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision taken.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Gap-led start, systems diagnosis, adaptive-capability lens.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Designed forward from content rather than backward from capability.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Balanced ~50/50 blend, genuinely integrated, within the time budget.`,
                    sum_r2_moreclass:       `Pushed the blend further into the classroom — the 70-20-10 inversion.`,
                    sum_r2_overbook:        `Overbooked the ~6-hour learner-time budget.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Blend or integration only partly aligned to how managers learn.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Spiral pathway, line manager engaged, deliverable-based gates.`,
                    sum_r3_nomgr:           `Left the line manager out of the design.`,
                    sum_r3_recall:          `Kept recall exams as the gate — evidences nothing about transfer.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Pathway sequencing only partly transfer-built.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Measured transfer, aligned assessment, pitched employer value.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Measured the wrong thing, lost completion, or pitched on prestige/price.`,

                    insight_integrated:     `Integrated pathway: you designed backward from Aurora’s real capability gaps, rebalanced toward how managers actually learn (≈70-20-10), and made the academic and workplace halves genuinely reinforce each other. Transfer rose, completion held, and the line manager was engineered into the design — so the uplift is real and Aurora has evidence it can trust.`,
                    insight_more_classroom: `The classroom trap: you answered a transfer problem with more of the content that wasn’t transferring, pushing the blend further from the 70-20-10 logic. Managers passed even more comfortably and changed even less — the design moved in the opposite direction to the one the gap demanded.`,
                    insight_content_led:    `Content-led to the end: the programme stayed organised around the modules you already owned rather than the capability gaps Aurora’s managers actually have. A well-sequenced set of topics is not the same as a pathway that closes a gap — so completion stayed high and transfer barely moved.`,
                    insight_overbooked:     `Overbooked pathway: the design assumed more learner time than managers — holding full jobs — could give. Half the beautiful pathway didn’t happen, engagement fell, and the 88% completion you were protecting slipped anyway. Design is allocation under a real constraint.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed redesign: some of the right moves landed, but the blend, integration, workplace engagement and measurement didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough seams that transfer rose only partway and Aurora is left unsure the uplift is durable.`,
                    insight_no_gapmap:      `You never mapped the capability gaps: without designing backward from what Aurora’s managers can’t yet do, the programme stayed content-led and you couldn’t tell the CHRO which specific gap each phase closes.`,
                    insight_no_integration: `Academic and workplace learning never truly integrated: they ran as separate tracks, so theory was never applied to the live problem and reflection never fed back into the theory — the core of Shelley’s pedagogy was left on the table.`,
                    insight_no_manager:     `You left out the line manager: transfer depends on workplace conditions — sponsorship, protected time, rewarded behaviour — and a pathway that ignores the boss fails the moment the learner returns to the job.`,
                    insight_no_measure:     `You never measured transfer directly: completion and satisfaction were always strong and were exactly what Aurora dismissed. Without manager-confirmed behaviour change, you can’t prove the uplift the CHRO demanded.`,
                    insight_completion_lost:`You let the 88% completion rate slip: protecting completion was never in tension with transfer once assessment became the workplace deliverable — losing it hands Aurora a programme that looks like it stopped working.`,

                    hint_overbook:          `Your design assumes more than the ~6 learner-hours managers have — half of it won’t happen and completion will fall. Cut the blend back to a feasible time budget before the next round.`,
                    hint_classroom:         `You’re answering a transfer gap with more classroom — the opposite of the 70-20-10 logic. Rebalance toward structured workplace application before transfer falls further.`,
                    hint_gapmap:            `Design is still running forward from content. Start from Aurora’s evaluation and map the specific capability gaps, so every later choice closes a real gap.`,
                    hint_integrate:         `The academic and workplace halves aren’t reinforcing each other. Integrate them so theory is applied to a live problem and reflection feeds back — that is where transfer comes from.`,
                    hint_manager:           `Transfer happens in the workplace. Engage the learner’s line manager as a sponsor — protected time, an agreed project, confirmed behaviour change — or the pathway fails on contact with the job.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — transfer up, completion held, Aurora reassured. Close it out: measure transfer directly and pitch employer-relevant value to renew the contract.`,

